Chapter: WorldSpec
  cites Foreword chapter ListUtils
  cites Foreword chapter Console
  cites Safari chapter Critter
  cites Safari chapter Trees
  cites Safari chapter Cat
  cites Safari chapter World
  cites Judge chapter Grade

 World: the route table, and the segment each row of it builds.

 THIS IS THE CHAPTER EVERY OTHER ONE READS, so the assertions here are about the
 route as a whole rather than about one segment. Nineteen rows, each carrying a
 length, a turn, and four flags; `segment-at` turns a row into everything the
 renderer needs, and the flags are what decide which populations exist.

 THE HEADINGS ARE GRADED IN DEGREES, WHICH IS HOW THE TABLE IS WRITTEN. Each
 segment's heading is the running sum of the previous rows' turns, so the whole
 accumulation -- and every sign in it -- is one list. Dividing the answer back by
 `deg` gets the degrees, which agree with the written turns to 1.4e-14, so they
 are graded a step rounded out from that. A chapter that took the absolute value
 without restoring the sign would give the same first row and nothing else.

 THE DISTRACTION RULE IS THE SUBTLE ONE. Nine of the nineteen segments carry
 pigs, and the FIRST TWO of them get the big seven-by-seven herd while the rest
 get two straight ranks -- the novelty wears off, which the chapter says in prose
 and computes with `pig-count-to (i + 1) <= pig-novelty-count`. It is an
 exclusive-looking test written inclusively over a count that includes the
 current segment, so an off-by-one either way changes which segments distract.
 The population SIZES are what catch that: 49 against 10.

 We say:

Section: The Table

  route-shape-got : List Integer
  route-shape-got =
    [ list-length route,
      pig-count-to 0 0, pig-count-to 3 0, pig-count-to 11 0, pig-count-to 12 0, pig-count-to 19 0 ]

  route-shape-want : List Integer
  route-shape-want = [19, 0, 1, 2, 3, 9]

 The turns as written, recovered from the accumulated headings. Segment 0 faces
 north; every later one carries the sum of the turns before it.

  headings-got : List Real
  headings-got =
    [ heading-at 0 / deg, heading-at 1 / deg, heading-at 2 / deg, heading-at 3 / deg,
      heading-at 4 / deg, heading-at 5 / deg, heading-at 6 / deg, heading-at 7 / deg,
      heading-at 8 / deg, heading-at 9 / deg, heading-at 10 / deg, heading-at 11 / deg,
      heading-at 12 / deg, heading-at 13 / deg, heading-at 14 / deg, heading-at 15 / deg,
      heading-at 16 / deg, heading-at 17 / deg, heading-at 18 / deg ]

  headings-want : List Real
  headings-want =
    [ 0.0, 50.0, -20.0, 0.0, 20.0, -50.0, -120.0, -40.0, -25.0, -95.0,
      -80.0, -65.0, -50.0, -35.0, -85.0, -35.0, -85.0, -35.0, -85.0 ]

  steps-got : List Real
  steps-got = [heading-step 0 / deg, heading-step 1 / deg, heading-step 6 / deg, heading-step 18 / deg]

  steps-want : List Real
  steps-want = [50.0, -70.0, 80.0, 0.0]

Section: A Segment

 Lengths come straight off the table and the lane is four metres everywhere.

  seg : Integer -> Segment
  seg (i) = segment-at i

  length-got : List Real
  length-got =
    [ (seg 0).length, (seg 1).length, (seg 6).length, (seg 9).length, (seg 18).length,
      (seg 0).width, (seg 18).width ]

  length-want : List Real
  length-want = [500.0, 320.0, 1200.0, 800.0, 300.0, 4.0, 4.0]

 Only the last segment terminates, and only it points its exit at itself; every
 other one points at the next. A right turn is a non-negative one, so the
 straight terminus counts as a right turn and it does not matter, because it has
 no turn to take.

  exit-got : List Integer
  exit-got = [(seg 0).exit-to, (seg 1).exit-to, (seg 17).exit-to, (seg 18).exit-to]

  exit-want : List Integer
  exit-want = [1, 2, 18, 18]

  flags-got : List Boolean
  flags-got =
    [ (seg 0).terminates, (seg 18).terminates,
      (seg 0).exit-right, (seg 1).exit-right, (seg 6).exit-right, (seg 18).exit-right,
      (seg 0).has-mid-tower, (seg 6).has-mid-tower, (seg 9).has-mid-tower,
      (seg 0).has-cat, (seg 1).has-cat, (seg 12).has-cat, (seg 18).has-cat ]

  flags-want : List Boolean
  flags-want =
    [ False, True,
      True, False, True, True,
      False, True, False,
      False, True, True, True ]

 The turn angle is the magnitude in radians, so a left and a right turn of the
 same size give the same angle and differ only in `exit-right`. The commit point
 is half a lane over its tangent before the end -- and the terminus commits at
 the end, because it has no turn.

 THE COMMIT POINT CARRIES `r-tan`'s ACCURACY. The wants below are the exact
 tangent; the game's is sine over cosine from a fidelity polynomial, and the
 difference grows with the angle because the tangent does: 5.6e-11 at fifty
 degrees, 1.7e-9 at seventy, 4.4e-9 at eighty. Measured, and graded a step out
 from the largest.

  angle-got : List Real
  angle-got =
    [ (seg 0).exit-angle / deg, (seg 1).exit-angle / deg,
      (seg 6).exit-angle / deg, (seg 18).exit-angle / deg,
      (seg 18).commit-along ]

  angle-want : List Real
  angle-want = [50.0, 70.0, 80.0, 0.0, 300.0]

  commit-got : List Real
  commit-got = [(seg 0).commit-along, (seg 1).commit-along, (seg 6).commit-along]

  commit-want : List Real
  commit-want = [498.32180073764545, 319.2720595314676, 1199.647346038583]

Section: The Populations

 Nine segments carry pigs and the first two of them get the herd. The sizes are
 what say which rule fired: forty-nine against ten, and nothing at all where the
 row has no pigs.

  pigs-got : List Integer
  pigs-got =
    [ list-length (seg 0).pigs, list-length (seg 2).pigs,
      list-length (seg 10).pigs, list-length (seg 11).pigs,
      list-length (seg 13).pigs, list-length (seg 18).pigs ]

  pigs-want : List Integer
  pigs-want = [0, 49, 49, 10, 10, 10]

  distract-got : List Boolean
  distract-got =
    [ (seg 0).pigs-distract, (seg 2).pigs-distract,
      (seg 10).pigs-distract, (seg 11).pigs-distract, (seg 18).pigs-distract ]

  distract-want : List Boolean
  distract-want = [False, True, True, False, False]

 A bull adds one to the herd, so a segment that has one carries fifteen cattle
 and one that does not carries fourteen.

  cows-got : List Integer
  cows-got = [list-length (seg 0).cows, list-length (seg 3).cows, list-length (seg 4).cows, list-length (seg 18).cows]

  cows-want : List Integer
  cows-want = [14, 14, 15, 15]

 Trees fill the segment in pairs from six metres in, every thirty metres, and
 stop eighty-five short of the end -- so a 300-metre segment gets seven pairs and
 a 1200-metre one thirty-seven. The cap at ninety-six is far above any row here;
 TreesSpec grades that boundary.

  trees-got : List Integer
  trees-got =
    [ list-length (seg 0).trees, list-length (seg 1).trees,
      list-length (seg 6).trees, list-length (seg 18).trees ]

  trees-want : List Integer
  trees-want = [28, 16, 74, 14]

 THE CAT IS BUILT FOR EVERY SEGMENT, not only the ones that have one -- the zig
 leaves the field undefined and Codex has no way to say that. So a segment
 without a cat still carries a well-formed one, and its across values are the
 chapter's constants rather than anything from the route.

  cat-got : List Real
  cat-got =
    [ (seg 0).cat.mid-across, (seg 18).cat.mid-across,
      (seg 0).cat.height, (seg 18).cat.height ]

  cat-want : List Real
  cat-want = [0.816, 0.816, 1.7, 1.7]

Section: Entry

  opening : [Console] Nothing = act
    print-line-uni (grade-ints  "wo-shape " route-shape-got route-shape-want)
    print-line-uni (grade-reals "wo-head  " headings-got headings-want 0.000000000001)
    print-line-uni (grade-reals "wo-step  " steps-got steps-want 0.000000000001)
    print-line-uni (grade-reals "wo-length" length-got length-want 0.0)
    print-line-uni (grade-ints  "wo-exit  " exit-got exit-want)
    print-line-uni (grade-bools "wo-flags " flags-got flags-want)
    print-line-uni (grade-reals "wo-angle " angle-got angle-want 0.000000000001)
    print-line-uni (grade-reals "wo-commit" commit-got commit-want 0.00000001)
    print-line-uni (grade-ints  "wo-pigs  " pigs-got pigs-want)
    print-line-uni (grade-bools "wo-distr " distract-got distract-want)
    print-line-uni (grade-ints  "wo-cows  " cows-got cows-want)
    print-line-uni (grade-ints  "wo-trees " trees-got trees-want)
    print-line-uni (grade-reals "wo-cat   " cat-got cat-want 0.0)
  end
